      "level": 2,
      "content": "Physical Divisions of WB"
    },
    {
      "id": "0kvnf2l",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Region</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Details</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Darjeeling Himalayan Hill Region</strong></p>",
            "<p>North — Darjeeling, Kalimpong; tea gardens; highest point Sandakphu (3636 m)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Terai &amp; Dooars</strong></p>",
            "<p>Sub-Himalayan foothill; dense forests; tea gardens; Jalpaiguri, Alipurduar</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>North Bengal Plains</strong></p>",
            "<p>Cooch Behar, Malda, Murshidabad; flat; agriculture</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Rarh Region</strong></p>",
            "<p>West — Bankura, Purulia, Birbhum; laterite soil; undulating terrain</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ganges Delta</strong></p>",
            "<p>South — fertile; rice cultivation; largest delta in the world</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Sundarbans</strong></p>",
            "<p>South — mangrove; Bay of Bengal coast; UNESCO World Heritage Site</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Coastal Region</strong></p>",
            "<p>Digha, Mandarmani, Bakkhali; Bay of Bengal shoreline</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0n57pmg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Major Rivers of West Bengal"
    },
    {
      "id": "9o7ng1c",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>River</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Facts</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ganga / Hooghly</strong></p>",
            "<p>Most important; enters WB from Bihar; Kolkata on its banks; joins Bay of Bengal</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Damodar</strong></p>",
            "<p>\"Sorrow of Bengal\" (due to floods); now controlled by DVC; Bardhaman-Hooghly belt</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Teesta</strong></p>",
            "<p>North Bengal; flows through Darjeeling; meets Brahmaputra in Bangladesh</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Mahananda</strong></p>",
            "<p>North Bengal; flows through Siliguri, Malda</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Jaldhaka &amp; Torsa</strong></p>",
            "<p>Dooars region; North Bengal</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Mayurakshi</strong></p>",
            "<p>Birbhum-Murshidabad belt</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Rupnarayan</strong></p>",
            "<p>Meets Hooghly near Tamluk; Midnapore region</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ichamati</strong></p>",
            "<p>South Bengal border area; near Bangladesh border</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Matla &amp; Bidyadhari</strong></p>",
            "<p>Sundarbans region; tidal rivers</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Kangsabati (Kasai)</strong></p>",
            "<p>Bankura-Midnapore; flows south to Bay of Bengal</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6tw2wwr",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>[EXAM TIP] </strong>Damodar river was called \"Sorrow of Bengal\" due to frequent floods. The Damodar Valley Corporation (DVC) was set up to control floods — it is India's first multipurpose river valley project.</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
